/*!
    \page opcua-client.html
    \title OPC UA Client
    \previouspage Getting Started
    \nextpage OPC UA Server

    The client half of the application connects to an OPC UA server, browses its address
    space, and reads, writes, subscribes to, and plots variable values. Under the hood
    the UI talks to the \l OpcUaManager facade, which delegates the network work to the
    worker-thread \l OpcUaService (see \l {Architecture}).

    \section1 Connecting to a server

    A connection is described by:

    \list
        \li a \b{discovery/endpoint URL}, for example \c {opc.tcp://127.0.0.1:4840};
        \li a \b{security policy} (for example \e None or \e Basic256Sha256 with signing
            and encryption);
        \li an \b{identity}: anonymous, or a username and password.
    \endlist

    Enter these in the connection form and connect. The client performs discovery and
    endpoint selection, then opens a session. Certificate-based security uses the
    application's PKI store (see \l {Configuration}). While connecting and after
    connecting, the state is shown in the status area and logged in the log panel.

    The \b{Discovery URL} field lists the ten most recently connected servers; you can
    also type a host, an IP address, or an \c {opc.tcp://} URL.

    \section1 Finding servers on the network

    If you do not know the address of a server, click \b{Scan network…} in the
    connection form. The scanner suggests the IPv4 subnets this PC is connected to, for
    example \c {10.10.1.0/24 (Ethernet 3)}. You can also enter a range such as
    \c {10.10.1.1-254}, \c {10.10.1.5-10.10.1.40}, or a single address. Only port
    \c {4840}, the standard OPC UA port, is checked by default; enter further ports as a
    list or range, for example \c {4840, 48010-48012}.

    One scan covers at most 1024 addresses and 16 ports. The scanner first checks which
    addresses accept a TCP connection and then asks each of them for its OPC UA server
    description and endpoints. Rows appear while the scan runs: an OPC UA server shows
    its name, security policies, and supported logins; a port that accepts connections
    but does not answer as an OPC UA server is shown dimmed.

    Double-click a server, or select it and click \b{Use}, to copy its address into the
    \b{Discovery URL} field. The client then finds the server and requests its endpoints
    automatically; choose the authentication and click \b{Connect}.

    \section1 Browsing the address space

    Once connected, the server's address space is shown as a tree of \e nodes, fetched
    lazily as you expand branches (backed by \l OpcUaModel). Selecting a node shows its
    \e attributes — node id, node class, data type, access level, value, and so on
    (\l AttributesModel).

    Attribute names and values can be selected with the mouse and copied with \c {Ctrl+C};
    \c {Ctrl+A} selects a whole field.

    Variable nodes whose values are structured (for example CODESYS structures that do
    not publish a full type definition) are decoded by browsing their instance subtree,
    so you see the individual fields rather than an opaque blob.

    \section1 Reading, writing, and subscribing

    \list
        \li \b Read — request the current value of a variable node.
        \li \b Write — send a new value to a writable variable (subject to its access
            level).
        \li \b Subscribe — register interest in a variable so the server pushes value
            changes. Subscribed variables appear in the \e{Data Access} view
            (\l DataAccessModel), which shows live value, status, and timestamp, and can
            be sorted and filtered (\l DataViewFilterModel).
    \endlist

    To add a variable to the \e{Data Access} view, tick its check box in the address space
    tree, or drag the node from the address space, or from a segment opened with
    \b{Open as segment}, and drop it onto the \b{DATA VIEW} panel. While you drag, a preview
    with the node's icon and name follows the pointer. On a touch screen, press and hold the
    node until its row is highlighted, then drag it; a plain swipe keeps scrolling the tree.
    A node that is already in the view is not added again.

    Unticking the check box removes the variable from the view. Removing a row from the view
    also clears the check box in the tree, so the node can be added again later.

    The \e{Context} column, right after \e{Display Name}, tells rows with the same name
    apart: it shows where the variable lives, in IEC notation starting at the GVL or program,
    for example \c{V_HMI.astTestPrepContainer[1].stRefOffset}. A narrow column keeps the end
    of the path visible; hover over it to see the full node path.

    The \b{#} column numbers the rows in the order they were added, and new rows are always
    appended at the end. Click a column header to sort by that column; click \b{#} to return
    to the order the rows were added in. The sort is not saved with the project.

    To change the order of the columns, drag the middle of a column header to the left or
    right; dragging near its edge resizes the column instead. The row number always stays
    first. Columns are shown or hidden with the eye button. The column layout is saved with
    the project and is also used as the starting layout of every new project, so the columns
    do not have to be arranged again.

    Monitored variables can be persisted so they are restored on the next connection
    (see \l {Configuration}).

    \section1 Saving a server snapshot

    \e{Project > Save Server Snapshot…} writes a read-only record of the connected
    server's address space to a \c{*.uasnapshot.json} file. Unlike a clone, a snapshot
    keeps everything as the server reports it: original NodeIds and namespaces, the data
    type of every variable with its base type, ValueRank, ArrayDimensions, access
    levels, and typed values with their status and timestamps. Nodes are sorted by
    browse path, so two snapshots of the same server can be compared with a text diff.

    The options choose the scope (the Objects folder, the node selected in the
    address-space tree with its subtree, or the whole server from the Root folder),
    whether values are read, the node limit and how many elements per array are kept.
    The snapshot uses the same traversal as the clone, with a progress dialog that can
    cancel it; a cancelled snapshot writes no file. A snapshot that reached a limit or
    could not read every node is still written, marked as incomplete in its
    \c completion section, and reported in a dialog. A snapshot may contain plant data;
    it is only written to the local file you choose.

    \section1 Trends

    Selected variables can be plotted over time in the trend panel. The trend keeps a
    bounded history of samples and computes the plot geometry (\l TrendModel); it can be
    paused and resumed.

    \section1 Logging

    Client activity — connection state, errors, and notable operations — is written to
    the log panel and to the application log file. The log file's folder can be opened
    from the UI, which is the starting point for \l {Troubleshooting}.
*/
